// n22 — the two fixed-point literals that are not fixed-point literals.
//
// CORBA 3.4 §7.2.6.5: `fixed_pt_literal` is digits, an optional point, more
// digits and a `d`. There is no exponent production, so `1e3d` is a floating
// literal wearing a suffix — omniidl 4.3.4 says "Cannot interpret floating
// point literal as fixed point" (measured 2026-08-21). Our lexer used to build
// the token by stripping the `d` and calling `f64::parse`, which accepted it
// and produced 1000.0: a value of the wrong type by a route that could not
// report anything, because the suffix had already been thrown away.
//
// §7.11.3 caps a `fixed` at 31 significant digits, and the second line has 32.
//
// **We are stricter than the oracle on the second line, deliberately.**
// omniidl rejects 32 *integer* digits and silently truncates 32 *fractional*
// digits to 31 — `0.1234…9012d` dumps back as `0.1234…901d`, measured the same
// day. Silently dropping a digit from a constant is the exact failure this
// batch exists to close, so both are refused here and the divergence is
// recorded in corpus/divergences.tsv.
//
// 지수부가 붙은 것은 fixed 리터럴이 아니다. 유효숫자는 31자리가 상한이며,
// 초과분을 조용히 버리는 것은 이 배치가 없애려는 바로 그 실패다.
module n22 {
  const fixed SCALED = 1e3d;
  const fixed TOO_MANY = 12345678901234567890123456789012d;
};
